When the UE receives application-layer buffer-level information from upper layers, it uses that information to populate the `appLayerBufferLevelList` carried in `UEAssistanceInformation`.

In principle, the UE:

- creates one `AppLayerBufferLevelInfo` entry for each application-layer buffer-level indication that is to be reported;
- associates the reported buffer level with the corresponding `appLayerId`;
- sets the `appLayerBufferLevel` field from the buffer-level value supplied by upper layers, using the encoding/range defined for that RRC IE;
- reports the current/latest indicated value—i.e. it does not derive the value from RLC/MAC buffer occupancy or attempt to combine it with lower-layer buffer status.

The UE must ensure that the value put into the RRC field is representable by the ASN.1-defined range and semantics of `appLayerBufferLevel`. Thus, if the upper-layer value needs quantisation or is above the highest representable level, the UE uses the applicable RRC encoding rule, including the maximum/saturated representation where defined. It does not send an out-of-range integer.

The list is only included when the relevant application-layer buffer-level reporting is applicable/triggered and the UE has buffer-level information to report. The UE also observes the list-size and application-ID constraints of `AppLayerBufferLevelList`; it cannot encode more entries than the RRC IE permits.

When a UE-to-Network Relay UE receives an `sl-ConfigDedicatedNR` that configures PC5 Relay RLC channels, it prepares an `RRCReconfigurationSidelink` for the applicable Remote UE(s) to convey the corresponding PC5-relay configuration.

In particular, the relay UE:

- identifies the PC5 Relay RLC channel configuration(s) in the received `sl-ConfigDedicatedNR` that apply to the PC5 link toward a Remote UE;
- includes the relevant PC5 Relay RLC channel add/modify configuration(s) in the `RRCReconfigurationSidelink` sent to that Remote UE;
- includes release information for PC5 Relay RLC channels that are released by the received configuration, where applicable;
- does not treat the PC5 Relay RLC channel as usable for the relayed traffic at the Remote UE until the sidelink reconfiguration procedure has been completed for that Remote UE.

Thus, the gNB-provided `sl-ConfigDedicatedNR` is used by the relay to drive the PC5-side reconfiguration: the relay forwards the applicable RLC-channel setup, modification, or release to the Remote UE through `RRCReconfigurationSidelink`, so that both ends of the PC5 relay link use consistent RLC-channel configurations.

The UE shall treat this as an **SCG reconfiguration failure**, not as a successful conditional reconfiguration and not as a configuration it may partially apply.

In (NG)EN-DC, when the RRCReconfiguration being applied was received on **SRB3** and the UE cannot comply with any part of it—including an RRCReconfiguration that is being executed from a `ConditionalReconfiguration`—the UE shall:

- regard execution of that conditional reconfiguration as failed;
- not send `RRCReconfigurationComplete` for that failed configuration;
- initiate the **SCG failure information procedure** toward the Master Node, using the surviving MCG signalling path (normally SRB1);
- report the failure as the applicable **SCG reconfiguration failure** cause (and include the relevant conditional-reconfiguration identity/failure information where applicable);
- perform the associated SCG-failure handling, i.e. stop relying on the inconsistent SCG configuration/release the SCG configuration as required by the SCG failure procedure.

The UE should therefore retain the MCG connection if it is still usable; failure of the conditional SRB3/SCG reconfiguration does not by itself mean that the UE silently accepts a partial configuration or necessarily triggers an MCG RRC re-establishment.

The mobility-history storage procedure is UE-wide and spans the UE’s mobility across **all RRC states**:

- **RRC_IDLE** — cells entered through cell selection/reselection;
- **RRC_INACTIVE** — cells entered through reselection while inactive;
- **RRC_CONNECTED** — cells entered, e.g., through handover or other connected-mode mobility.

Thus, it is not limited to a single connected-mode handover transaction. The UE accumulates the history of visited cells as it moves between cells, subject to the specification’s retention/list-size rules. The network may subsequently request/report that stored history when the relevant RRC signalling permits it.

In `RRC_CONNECTED`, the UE determines its RRC mobility measurements from the currently applicable **`MeasConfig`**. The network provides or updates this configuration, normally in `RRCReconfiguration` (and, where applicable, other RRC procedures carrying a measurement configuration).

The UE does not simply measure every detectable cell/frequency for RRC reporting purposes. It applies the configured measurement objects, reporting rules, identities, quantities, and measurement restrictions.

## 1. Measurement object: what to measure

A **measurement object** (`MeasObject`) tells the UE the target to be measured, for example:

- an NR carrier/frequency (`MeasObjectNR`);
- an E-UTRA carrier/frequency (`MeasObjectEUTRA`), where configured.

For an NR object, the configuration can indicate, among other things:

- the NR frequency and subcarrier spacing;
- whether measurements use **SSB** and/or **CSI-RS**;
- configured cells and their cell-specific offsets;
- cells/PCI ranges to exclude or blacklist;
- whether particular cells are allowed or preferred for the object;
- the quantity-configuration index to use.

Thus, the measurement object defines the frequency/RAT and the reference signals/cells relevant to the measurement.

## 2. Measurement identity: which object is associated with which reporting rule

The UE uses each configured **measurement identity** (`MeasId`) to associate:

- one `MeasObject`, and
- one `ReportConfig`.

Conceptually:

\[
\text{MeasId} \rightarrow (\text{MeasObject}, \text{ReportConfig})
\]

A measurement object may be associated with multiple report configurations through multiple measurement identities. For example, the same NR frequency can be measured for both:

- an event-triggered A3 report, and
- a periodic report.

A measurement object which is not associated with an active `MeasId` does not provide an RRC measurement-reporting configuration through that identity association.

## 3. Report configuration: when and what to report

The associated **report configuration** (`ReportConfigNR` or the applicable inter-RAT report configuration) determines how the UE uses the measurements. It specifies such matters as:

- event-triggered versus periodic reporting;
- the event conditions, such as A1, A2, A3, A4, A5, A6, B1, or B2, as applicable;
- thresholds, offsets, hysteresis, and time-to-trigger;
- report interval and report amount for periodic reporting;
- maximum number of cells to include;
- whether to report cell-level and/or beam-level results;
- the measurement quantities to include, such as RSRP, RSRQ, and SINR.

For event-triggered reporting, the UE evaluates the configured event using the applicable serving-cell and neighbour-cell measurement results. It sends a `MeasurementReport` only when the entry/leaving/reporting conditions of that report configuration are met.

## 4. Quantity configuration: how measurements are processed

`QuantityConfig` provides the measurement-processing rules, including relevant Layer-3 filtering configuration for quantities such as:

- SSB-RSRP, SSB-RSRQ, SSB-SINR;
- CSI-RS-RSRP, CSI-RS-RSRQ, CSI-RS-SINR;
- corresponding inter-RAT quantities where relevant.

The UE uses the configured filtering before evaluating event conditions and producing reported results. Therefore, event triggering is generally based on the applicable filtered measurement quantities, not merely on an instantaneous PHY sample.

## 5. Measurement gaps and feasibility

The UE performs configured measurements subject to the measurement opportunities available to it, including:

- configured **measurement gaps**, when needed;
- its RF/measurement capabilities;
- serving-cell activity and other applicable RRC/MAC/PHY constraints;
- availability of the configured SSB or CSI-RS measurement resources.

Measurement-gap configuration enables measurements that cannot be performed concurrently with serving-cell reception/transmission, especially for inter-frequency or inter-RAT measurements. Absence of a gap does not necessarily mean that no such measurement is possible; a capable UE may perform it without a gap. But the UE cannot be expected to make measurements that are infeasible under its capabilities and the configured opportunities.

## 6. `s-Measure` can limit inter-frequency/inter-RAT measurement activity

If `s-MeasureConfig` is configured, it can be used to control whether the UE needs to perform certain inter-frequency and/or inter-RAT measurements based on the serving-cell signal level.

In principle, when the serving-cell measurement is above the configured `s-Measure` threshold, the UE may not be required to perform the relevant non-serving-frequency measurements. When the serving-cell quality falls below that threshold, the UE performs those measurements as configured. The precise applicability depends on the configured measurement type and associated rules.

## 7. Configuration is maintained until changed or released

The UE maintains the measurement configuration in `RRC_CONNECTED` until it is modified or released. The network can use add/modify/release lists for:

- measurement objects;
- report configurations;
- measurement identities;
- quantity configurations;
- measurement gaps;
- `s-Measure` configuration.

Therefore, the UE determines the active measurement set from the resulting current configuration, not only from the most recently received individual list.

## Important distinction

This concerns **RRC measurement reporting for mobility and network control**. The UE may also perform other physical-layer measurements needed for functions such as beam management, link monitoring, radio link failure detection, beam failure recovery, or CSI processing. Those measurements are not necessarily identical to the measurements configured for `MeasurementReport` generation.

In short: in `RRC_CONNECTED`, the UE performs RRC measurements according to the active `MeasConfig`; `MeasObject` identifies what can be measured, `MeasId` associates it with a reporting rule, `ReportConfig` determines when and what is reported, and `QuantityConfig`, measurement-gap configuration, serving conditions, and UE capability determine how and when those measurements are practically performed.

The purpose is to enable and control the UE’s role as an **L2 UE-to-Network (U2N) relay**.

By providing relay-operation-related configuration, the network instructs an eligible UE how to support remote UE traffic over sidelink and forward that traffic toward/from the network over its Uu connection. In particular, the configuration is used to:

- enable the UE to operate as an L2 relay for one or more remote UEs;
- configure the sidelink/PC5 resources, bearers and associated parameters needed for the relay-facing radio link;
- configure the association between traffic received on PC5 and the applicable Uu-side transport/bearer handling;
- allow the relay UE to forward both user-plane traffic and, where applicable, control-plane/RRC-related traffic for the remote UE;
- let the network control relay availability, supported services, radio-resource use, and the conditions under which relay operation is permitted.

The relay UE is still a UE from the network’s perspective; it does not become a gNB. Its L2 relay function is to provide the forwarding path between the remote UE’s sidelink connection and the network-facing Uu connection. Configuration alone does not mean that traffic is immediately relayed: relay discovery/selection and establishment of the relevant PC5 and network-side connections must also occur.

For a **periodical CSI report** where exactly one reporting quantity is set to `true` in `reportQuantityRS-Indexes`, that enabled quantity is the **sorting quantity**.

Thus, the UE ranks the applicable RS resources/indexes using the corresponding Layer-1 measurement quantity:

- only `rsrp` set to `true` → sort by **L1-RSRP**;
- only `rsrq` set to `true` → sort by **L1-RSRQ**;
- only `sinr` set to `true` → sort by **L1-SINR**.

The RS indexes reported are therefore selected/ordered according to that single configured quantity, normally with the highest measured quality ranked first. There is no need for a priority rule or combination of quantities when only one is enabled.

The **SCGFailureInformation** procedure is the UE-to-Master Node (MN) reporting mechanism for a failure of the **Secondary Cell Group (SCG)** in an MR-DC configuration.

When the UE detects an SCG failure—for example, due to SCG radio link failure, an SCG random-access problem, or an SCG RLC maximum-retransmission condition—it reports this to the MN by sending an **SCGFailureInformation** message over the MCG-side signalling radio bearer (normally SRB1).

Its purposes are to:

- inform the MN that the UE can no longer reliably use the SCG;
- provide the failure cause/type and, where applicable, related measurement/failure information;
- allow the MN to decide the recovery action, such as SCG reconfiguration, SCG release, secondary-node change, or other MR-DC configuration changes;
- ensure the UE does not continue normal use of the failed SCG while it remains connected through the MCG.

Thus, it is not itself an SCG recovery procedure. It is the notification that enables the MN, which controls the overall MR-DC connection, to initiate the appropriate recovery or reconfiguration action.

The network controls UE measurement reporting by sending a **measurement configuration** (`MeasConfig`) to the UE, normally in `RRCReconfiguration`. The UE does not autonomously report every measured cell or beam; it reports only according to the configured measurement identities and reporting rules.

The main control mechanism is:

\[
\text{MeasId} \rightarrow (\text{MeasObject},\ \text{ReportConfig})
\]

Each `measId` associates:

- a **measurement object**: *what* the UE shall measure; and
- a **report configuration**: *when, what, and how often* the UE shall report.

## 1. Control of what the UE measures

The network configures one or more measurement objects, for example `MeasObjectNR` or an inter-RAT measurement object. These identify such items as:

- NR carrier/frequency and applicable SSB/CSI-RS measurement configuration;
- cells, PCI lists, allowed-cell lists, or excluded/blacklisted cells;
- cell-specific offsets;
- reference signal type and related measurement details;
- possibly another RAT/frequency for inter-frequency or inter-RAT mobility.

The network may also configure measurement gaps (`measGapConfig`) when the UE needs time to measure frequencies or RATs not measurable concurrently with its serving carrier.

Further, quantity configuration controls measurement filtering, e.g. L3 filter coefficients for RSRP, RSRQ and SINR. Thus event evaluation is generally based on filtered measurement quantities, not necessarily an instantaneous radio sample.

## 2. Control of when the UE reports

The network configures a `ReportConfig`, such as `ReportConfigNR`. It can request:

- **Event-triggered reporting**, or
- **Periodic reporting**, or
- special reporting such as **CGI reporting**.

### Event-triggered reporting

For event reporting, the network configures conditions such as NR events A1 through A6, and inter-RAT events where applicable. Examples include:

- serving cell becomes better than a threshold;
- serving cell becomes worse than a threshold;
- neighbour becomes better than a threshold;
- neighbour becomes better than the serving cell by an offset;
- a secondary-cell-related event.

The network controls the trigger behavior through parameters including:

- threshold(s);
- cell/measurement offsets;
- hysteresis;
- `timeToTrigger`;
- trigger quantity, such as RSRP, RSRQ or SINR;
- whether the condition is evaluated for serving and/or neighbour cells.

The UE sends a `MeasurementReport` only after the configured event condition has remained satisfied for the configured `timeToTrigger`, subject to the measurement configuration.

For event reporting, the network also controls:

- `reportInterval`: spacing between subsequent reports;
- `reportAmount`: finite number of reports or effectively unlimited reporting;
- `reportOnLeave`: whether to report when the triggering condition ceases to be true;
- `maxReportCells`: maximum number of cells included in a report.

Thus, an event can cause one report, a limited sequence of reports, or continuing reports while the event remains valid.

### Periodic reporting

For periodic reporting, the network configures the reporting interval and the number of reports. The UE then sends reports periodically for the corresponding `measId`, rather than waiting for a radio event.

## 3. Control of what is included in a MeasurementReport

The report configuration also controls the report content, for example:

- cell measurement quantities to report, such as RSRP, RSRQ and/or SINR;
- maximum number of neighbour cells;
- SSB or CSI-RS based reporting;
- RS-index/beam measurement results;
- whether beam measurements are included;
- CGI-related information where a CGI report is configured.

Therefore, the network can request only cell-level results, or more detailed RS/beam-level results when needed.

## 4. Control of whether inter-frequency measurements are needed

The network can configure `s-Measure` behavior. This can limit when the UE is required to perform inter-frequency measurements, for example by making such measurements unnecessary while the serving-cell quality is sufficiently good. Together with measurement gaps and frequency-specific measurement objects, this controls both UE measurement effort and the availability of reportable results.

## 5. Starting, modifying, and stopping reporting

The network can dynamically control reporting by adding, modifying, or removing:

- measurement objects;
- report configurations;
- measurement identities (`measId`);
- measurement gaps;
- quantity/filter configurations.

Removing a `measId`, or removing/replacing its associated report configuration, stops the corresponding reporting behavior. A finite `reportAmount` also naturally terminates reporting after the configured number of reports.

## 6. UE behavior and RRC state

The UE sends the resulting `MeasurementReport` on the RRC signalling connection, normally on SRB1 while in `RRC_CONNECTED`. Measurements may also be used locally for cell selection/reselection in non-connected states, but this does not mean the UE sends normal connected-mode `MeasurementReport` messages to the network.

In summary, the network controls measurement reporting through `MeasConfig`: it selects the frequencies/cells/signals to measure, maps them to reporting rules using `measId`, defines event or periodic trigger conditions, controls report frequency and duration, and specifies the measurement results to include.

The UE does not remain connected to NR waiting for E-UTRA to appear. On reception of the `RRCRelease` carrying `voiceFallbackIndication`, it is released to `RRC_IDLE` and first attempts the intended E-UTRA selection/redirection for EPS fallback.

If no suitable E-UTRA cell can be selected, the UE falls back to normal NR cell-selection behavior:

- it performs/selects a suitable NR cell in accordance with the NR idle-mode cell-selection rules;
- if an acceptable NR cell is found, it camps on that NR cell in `RRC_IDLE`;
- any further attempt to establish RRC—for example, to continue/retry the pending voice-related NAS procedure—is made through the normal RRC connection-establishment procedure, as triggered by upper layers;
- if no suitable NR cell is found either, the UE remains out of service and continues normal cell-selection/search procedures.

In particular, this is not an RRC re-establishment case: failure to find E-UTRA after a voice-fallback release does not itself cause `RRCReestablishment`; the recovery is return to normal idle-mode NR selection.

In NR, **RRC controls the configuration of user-plane radio bearers**, but it does not itself carry the user payload. User data is carried on **Data Radio Bearers (DRBs)** through the PDCP/RLC/MAC/PHY stack. RRC establishes, modifies, suspends, re-establishes, and releases those DRBs.

## 1. Bearer types

RRC distinguishes:

- **Signalling Radio Bearers (SRBs):** carry RRC and NAS signalling.
  - SRB0 is used for initial common-control signalling.
  - SRB1 is established during RRC connection setup and is used for dedicated signalling.
  - SRB2 is normally added later for further dedicated signalling.
- **Data Radio Bearers (DRBs):** carry user-plane IP/Ethernet data associated with PDU sessions and QoS flows.

Thus, an RRC connection may exist with SRBs only; DRBs are added only when user-plane service is required.

## 2. When DRBs are established

Typically, the UE first completes:

1. Random access and initial RRC signalling;
2. `RRCSetup` / `RRCSetupComplete`, entering `RRC_CONNECTED`;
3. NAS registration and PDU session establishment;
4. AS security activation, normally through `SecurityModeCommand` / `SecurityModeComplete`.

When the network decides that user-plane resources are needed—for example, following a PDU Session Resource Setup request from the 5GC—the gNB configures DRBs, normally using an **`RRCReconfiguration`** message.

There is no separate “DRB setup” RRC message. DRB establishment is an outcome of applying the `RadioBearerConfig` and associated cell-group configuration carried by RRC reconfiguration.

The UE confirms successful processing with `RRCReconfigurationComplete`.

## 3. What RRC configures for a DRB

A DRB configuration identifies the bearer and connects the protocol layers needed to transport data. Key aspects include:

- **DRB identity** (`drb-Identity`);
- Association with the core-network service:
  - normally an **SDAP configuration**, including mapping between QoS Flows/QFIs and the DRB;
  - or, where applicable, an EPS bearer identity for interworking;
- **PDCP configuration**, such as:
  - PDCP sequence number size;
  - header compression configuration;
  - ciphering/integrity-related operation;
  - duplication, reordering, discard and recovery behavior where configured;
  - whether PDCP should be re-established or recovered after certain procedures;
- **RLC bearer configuration**, including AM, UM, or other applicable RLC behavior;
- **MAC logical-channel configuration**, including:
  - logical channel priority;
  - prioritized bit rate;
  - bucket size/duration;
  - logical channel group;
  - scheduling request-related behavior;
- Association with the relevant **cell group** and, where configured, multiple transmission legs or split-bearer operation.

Conceptually, the bearer path is:

`QoS Flow(s) ↔ SDAP ↔ PDCP ↔ RLC ↔ MAC ↔ PHY`

RRC configures that path; PDCP, RLC, MAC, and PHY then perform the actual packet transport.

## 4. Relationship between QoS flows and DRBs

In 5G, the 5GC provides QoS in terms of **QoS Flows**, identified by QFIs. The gNB determines how those flows are mapped to DRBs.

A DRB can carry:

- one QoS flow; or
- multiple QoS flows with compatible radio treatment.

SDAP performs the QoS-flow-to-DRB mapping. In downlink, the QFI can be indicated to the UE; in uplink, SDAP uses the configured mapping to select the appropriate DRB for outgoing user packets.

RRC therefore configures the radio-side realization of the QoS policy, while the scheduler and lower layers enforce the actual radio-resource treatment.

## 5. Addition, modification, and release

RRC can manage DRBs dynamically while the UE remains in `RRC_CONNECTED`.

### Add

The network includes a DRB in the appropriate add/modify list. The UE creates or configures the relevant PDCP entity, RLC bearer, logical channel, and SDAP association before using the bearer for traffic.

### Modify

The network may reconfigure an existing DRB, for example to change:

- QoS-flow mapping;
- RLC mode or parameters;
- PDCP parameters;
- logical-channel priority or bit-rate treatment;
- serving cell/cell-group association;
- duplication or split-bearer operation.

This permits adaptation to mobility, load, changing QoS requirements, dual connectivity, or PDU-session changes.

### Release

The network places the DRB identity in a release list. The UE stops using that bearer and releases the corresponding lower-layer resources as directed by the configuration.

A DRB may be released because:

- the PDU session or QoS flow is released;
- radio resources are no longer needed;
- the UE moves to another configuration;
- dual-connectivity resources are removed;
- the RRC connection is fully released.

Releasing a DRB is distinct from releasing the entire RRC connection: the UE can remain `RRC_CONNECTED` with SRBs and other DRBs still active.

## 6. Security handling

User-plane DRBs are normally protected by Access Stratum security. After security mode activation, PDCP applies the configured ciphering and, where applicable, integrity protection behavior.

RRC provides the configuration and triggering framework, while PDCP uses the active AS security keys and PDCP COUNT/state to protect packets. Security context continuity and PDCP state handling become particularly important during resume, handover, re-establishment, and dual-connectivity changes.

## 7. Behavior in RRC states

### `RRC_CONNECTED`

DRBs may be configured and actively carry user data. RRC can add, modify, or release them through dedicated signalling.

### `RRC_INACTIVE`

The UE does not normally have continuously active user-plane radio resources. With `RRCRelease` including suspend information, the UE may retain the relevant AS context, including information needed for efficient resume.

On `RRCResume`, the network can restore/reuse the suspended context and can send further RRC reconfiguration if the bearer setup or mapping must change.

### `RRC_IDLE`

There is no active dedicated radio bearer configuration for immediate user-plane transfer. A new connection/resume-related procedure is needed before DRB user data can again be sent over the radio interface.

## 8. Re-establishment and failure recovery

After radio link failure or certain other failures, the UE may perform RRC re-establishment. The outcome for DRBs depends on the received configuration and on bearer-specific settings, including whether PDCP is to be re-established or recovered.

RRC and PDCP configuration determine such matters as:

- whether PDCP sequence-number/count state is retained, recovered, or reset;
- whether retransmission/recovery mechanisms are used;
- whether RLC entities are re-established;
- whether the network must reconfigure the bearers after re-establishment.

The network may restore bearer operation through subsequent `RRCReconfiguration`, or it may release/recreate DRBs if continuity cannot be maintained.

## 9. Mobility and multi-connectivity

During handover or dual connectivity, RRC may move or reconfigure DRBs between cell groups, configure split bearers, or change the PDCP/RLC placement. This enables traffic to use the master cell group, secondary cell group, or more than one radio leg, depending on the configured architecture.

The DRB identity and QoS relationship can remain logically meaningful while the radio resources and lower-layer entities are changed.

## Summary

RRC manages user-data bearers by configuring **DRBs** and their associated SDAP, PDCP, RLC, MAC, and radio-resource parameters. DRBs are generally added or changed through `RRCReconfiguration`, are secured through the AS security/PDCP framework, map QoS flows to radio resources, and can be suspended, resumed, re-established, moved, or released as the UE state, QoS needs, mobility situation, and network resource conditions change.

RRC is involved because measurement gaps are a **network-controlled interruption of normal serving-cell operation**. A UE may need time to retune its RF chain, synchronize to, and measure another NR frequency or another RAT (for example LTE), while it cannot simultaneously receive/transmit on the serving cell. The network therefore cannot leave this purely to UE implementation; it must explicitly configure when such interruptions are allowed.

In TS 38.331, measurement-gap configuration is part of the RRC measurement configuration framework (for example through `MeasGapConfig` in `MeasConfig`). RRC can configure parameters such as the gap pattern/length, repetition periodicity, timing offset, and associated timing adjustment. This tells the UE exactly which periods are measurement gaps.

The reason for RRC control is that, during a gap:

- the UE may be unable to receive scheduled downlink data/control on the serving cell;
- the UE may be unable to transmit uplink data/control;
- the gNB scheduler must avoid expecting normal UE availability during the gap;
- the gap timing must be coordinated with the serving-cell timing and with other configured functions.

A measurement gap is especially relevant when the UE does not have sufficient parallel RF capability to measure a target frequency/RAT while maintaining normal service on the serving frequency. A UE with more capable radio hardware may be able to perform some measurements without gaps, but RRC provides the standardized network mechanism when gaps are needed.

The broader functional context is **RRC-controlled radio resource management and mobility measurement**. RRC configures:

- what the UE is to measure (`MeasObject`);
- how and when results are reported (`ReportConfig`, `MeasId`);
- measurement quantities and filtering (`QuantityConfig`);
- and, where necessary, the time opportunities in which off-serving-frequency or inter-RAT measurements can be made (`MeasGapConfig`).

The resulting measurement reports are used by the network for functions such as inter-frequency mobility, inter-RAT mobility, handover preparation and execution, carrier/frequency management, and other RRM decisions. Thus, measurement gaps are not primarily a data-plane feature; they are an RRC mobility/RRM mechanism that balances the need to discover and assess target cells against continuity of service on the serving cell.

After an RRC resume failure, the UE shall create/update `VarConnEstFailReport` for the failed resume attempt. The report contains, in particular:

- **`plmn-Identity`** — the PLMN associated with the cell on which the UE attempted the resume;
- **`failedPCellId`** — the physical cell ID of that failed PCell;
- **`measResultFailedCell`** — the available radio measurement result for the failed cell, e.g. NR RSRP/RSRQ/SINR as applicable;
- **`timeConnEstFail`** — the time information associated with the connection-establishment/resume failure;
- **`connEstFailCause`** — indicating that the failure was an **RRC resume failure**.

Release-specific extensions can add further information, but the above is the core failure-report content.

The `plmn-Identity` is not inferred from the RRC resume identity or from the old inactive AS context. It is obtained from the **PLMN information broadcast by the cell on which the UE sent the `RRCResumeRequest`**—i.e. the applicable PLMN identity in that cell’s SIB1 `plmn-IdentityList`, corresponding to the PLMN on which the UE is camped/performing the resume.

`gapSharingFR2 = setup` is not, by itself, a command to create or activate an FR2 measurement gap.

If the UE has no active FR2 measurement-gap-sharing configuration to which the indication can apply, the UE has no shared FR2 gap resource to use. Therefore:

- the UE does not generate a new FR2 measurement gap merely because `gapSharingFR2` is set to `setup`;
- it does not start sharing gaps, suspend serving-cell activity, or perform additional inter-frequency/inter-RAT measurements on that basis;
- the gap-sharing indication is effectively inapplicable until an FR2 gap-sharing configuration is active;
- any measurements continue according to other applicable measurement-gap configuration, measurement timing configuration, and UE measurement capabilities.

Thus, `gapSharingFR2` acts as an enable/use indication for an existing applicable FR2 gap-sharing configuration; it has no operative gap-sharing effect when such a configuration is absent.

The **UEInformationRequest procedure is network-initiated**. There is no UE-side event that automatically starts this procedure.

When the gNB wants UE-held diagnostic, measurement, or history information, it sends an **`UEInformationRequest`** RRC message to the UE on **DL-DCCH** while the UE is in **RRC_CONNECTED** (normally using SRB1). The request contains one or more request indications specifying the information wanted.

Typical information the network may request includes, depending on the configured/requested fields:

- **RLF report** — information related to a previous radio link failure and/or re-establishment;
- **RACH report** — random-access related information;
- **Logged measurement report** — MDT/logged measurements previously collected by the UE;
- **Connection establishment failure report**;
- **Mobility history information**.

The actual trigger is an **implementation/network decision**, for example:

- radio-link-failure or re-establishment troubleshooting;
- mobility robustness optimisation;
- MDT/trace collection;
- random-access or connection-establishment failure investigation;
- SON/OAM diagnostics.

Upon receiving `UEInformationRequest`, the UE checks the requested items and whether corresponding valid information is available. It then returns a **`UEInformationResponse`** containing the requested available reports. If a report is unavailable, expired, invalid, or not applicable, the UE does not provide that report as specified for that information type.

Therefore, the key point is: **the network triggers the procedure explicitly by sending `UEInformationRequest` to a connected UE; it is not automatically triggered by the UE detecting RLF, RACH failure, or another event.**

For a UE in **RRC_CONNECTED**, the network provides or updates the measurement configuration using an **`RRCReconfiguration`** message, via the optional **`measConfig`** IE.

`measConfig` can configure, modify, or release measurement-related entities, including for example:

- Measurement objects (`measObjectNR`, `measObjectEUTRA`, etc.), i.e. frequencies/cells to measure;
- Reporting configurations (`reportConfigNR`, `reportConfigInterRAT`), including event- or periodical-triggered reporting;
- Measurement identities (`measId`), which associate a measurement object with a reporting configuration;
- Quantity configuration, e.g. RSRP/RSRQ/SINR filtering and reporting quantities;
- Measurement gaps, if required;
- S-measure and speed-state related measurement behavior;
- Other measurement-control parameters applicable to the configured RATs.

The configuration is generally **delta-based**: the network may add, modify, or release only selected measurement objects, report configurations, or measurement identities. If the `measConfig` IE is absent in an `RRCReconfiguration`, the UE keeps its existing measurement configuration unless another applicable instruction changes it.

After receiving and applying the `RRCReconfiguration`, the UE performs the configured measurements and sends `MeasurementReport` messages when the relevant configured reporting criteria are met.

An `RRCResume` may also carry measurement configuration when a UE is resumed from `RRC_INACTIVE` into `RRC_CONNECTED`; however, for an already connected UE, the normal mechanism is `RRCReconfiguration`.

A UE uses the `sl-RxPool` broadcast in `SIB12` as the **cell-common NR sidelink discovery receive pool** when all of the following apply:

- The UE is **camped on the cell** and has acquired a valid `SIB12`;
- `sl-RxPool` is **present/configured** in that SIB12;
- The UE is **enabled/authorized by upper layers to perform NR sidelink discovery reception** and has an interest in monitoring discovery messages;
- The UE is using the **cell-common sidelink configuration**—in particular, this is the normal basis for discovery monitoring in `RRC_IDLE` or `RRC_INACTIVE`.

The UE then monitors only the time/frequency resources, numerology/BWP, and other pool parameters defined by that `sl-RxPool`, and applies its configured discovery identity/filtering when deciding whether a received sidelink message is relevant.

The presence of `sl-RxPool` does **not** mean that every UE continuously decodes all sidelink traffic in the pool. It is used for discovery monitoring only by UEs that are configured/interested in sidelink discovery. If an applicable dedicated sidelink/discovery receive-resource configuration is provided to an `RRC_CONNECTED` UE, that dedicated configuration governs the UE’s reception rather than treating the SIB12 pool as an unconditional receive pool.

If an `RRCReject` contains `waitTime`, the UE treats the RRC setup attempt as rejected and:

- starts (or restarts) **timer T302** with the value indicated by `waitTime`;
- returns/remains in **RRC_IDLE** as part of handling the failed RRC setup;
- while **T302 is running**, the UE does not initiate a new normal RRC connection establishment / `RRCSetupRequest` attempt.

Thus, `waitTime` is a network-provided retry back-off time, typically used for congestion control. It is not merely informational and does not keep the UE in a connected state.

When T302 expires, the UE may again initiate RRC setup if there is an upper-layer request and normal access conditions are satisfied (cell selection, access barring/UAC, etc.). Any explicitly specified exceptional access, such as emergency access where applicable, is handled according to the corresponding RRC access rules rather than as an ordinary retry.

If the UE receives an `RRCReject` in response to an `RRCResumeRequest` or `RRCResumeRequest1`, and that resume procedure was initiated for an RNA update, the UE treats the resume as failed and shall:

- stop `T319`;
- reset MAC;
- enter **RRC_IDLE** — i.e. it does **not** return to/remain in `RRC_INACTIVE` for this case; and
- perform cell selection according to the RRC idle-mode cell-selection procedure.

Any `waitTime`/related barring information carried in the `RRCReject` is also handled as specified (for example, by starting the applicable timer).

An IAB-MT initiates the **IAB Other Information** procedure to convey IAB-node-specific information to its **IAB-donor-CU** when that information becomes available or changes and the donor CU needs it for IAB-node management.

The main reason is to report the IAB node’s **IAB-node address information** (for example, an address used to identify/reach the IAB node in the IAB transport/topology context). Typical situations include:

- the IAB node has obtained its address for the first time;
- the reported IAB-node address has changed;
- an IAB-node recovery, re-establishment, re-parenting, or transport/topology change has resulted in new reportable IAB information;
- the donor-CU has configured/requested such reporting.

The message is sent by the **MT function** because the MT is the RRC endpoint towards the donor-CU, while the information concerns the overall IAB node/DU side as well. It lets the donor-CU maintain the association between the IAB-MT, the IAB-DU, and the donor’s IAB topology/transport context.

It is therefore not a normal UE assistance or measurement report. It is an IAB-specific uplink RRC procedure, performed when the MT is able to communicate with the donor-CU—normally in `RRC_CONNECTED`—and there is reportable IAB other information to provide.

An **MCCH information change notification is only an indication that the currently stored MCCH information may no longer be valid**. It is not the updated MCCH content itself and normally does not identify which specific MCCH parameter or MBS session changed.

Therefore, after detecting the notification, the UE reacquires MCCH so that it can obtain the current multicast/broadcast control information and act on it correctly.

This is necessary because MCCH information can affect, for example:

- which MBS services/sessions are being transmitted;
- whether a service of interest was added, removed, started, stopped, or reconfigured;
- the mapping/configuration needed to receive the associated multicast traffic channel(s);
- scheduling and radio-resource-related information for MBS reception;
- identifiers and other control information used to determine whether the UE should receive a particular service.

Without reacquisition, the UE could continue using stale MCCH information. That could cause it to:

- try to decode traffic in no-longer-valid resources;
- miss a newly available or reconfigured service;
- continue monitoring/receiving a service that has been removed or changed; or
- apply an obsolete service-to-radio-resource mapping.

Thus, the notification is a synchronization trigger: it tells the UE to read MCCH again at the applicable MCCH acquisition opportunity, so the UE’s MBS service and reception configuration is aligned with the network’s current configuration.

The UE does this to establish a **baseline** for change-based SS-RSRP reporting of the SpCell.

The “reference SS-RSRP” is an internal stored value, not a new radio measurement configuration and not a replacement for the measured SS-RSRP. By setting it to the **current** SS-RSRP of the SpCell, the UE starts the relevant monitoring/reporting procedure from the radio condition that exists at that moment.

Subsequent SpCell SS-RSRP measurements can then be compared against that reference value. If the configured SS-RSRP-change criterion is met—i.e. the change from the stored reference exceeds the configured threshold—the UE can perform the corresponding reporting/action and, where specified, refresh the reference value.

This is needed so that the UE:

- evaluates a **change** in serving-cell radio conditions, rather than an absolute SS-RSRP level;
- does not use an obsolete SS-RSRP value from before a configuration change, re-establishment, or prior reporting cycle;
- avoids immediately treating an already-existing low/high SS-RSRP as a newly occurred change;
- provides the network an indication when the SpCell’s radio quality has materially changed since the applicable reference point.

In short, the current SpCell SS-RSRP is stored as the reference so that later SS-RSRP variation can be measured relative to a valid, current baseline.

A UE initiates the **SCG failure information procedure** in an MR-DC/DC configuration when it determines that the **Secondary Cell Group (SCG) can no longer be reliably used**, while the Master Cell Group (MCG) connection is still available.

The purpose is to inform the **Master Node (MN)** that the secondary leg has failed, so that the MN can take recovery action—for example, release the SCG, reconfigure bearers, request recovery/re-addition of the SCG, or select another SN/SCG.

Typical triggering situations include an SCG failure being declared due to, for example:

- **SCG radio link failure**, such as expiry of the applicable `T310` after out-of-sync indications;
- a **random-access problem** on the SCG;
- **RLC maximum retransmission** failure associated with the SCG;
- failure during an **SCG change**, synchronization/reconfiguration, or other SCG establishment/reconfiguration-related failure, where applicable;
- other lower-layer indications that meet the specification’s SCG-failure criteria.

The UE reports the failure using **`SCGFailureInformation`**, normally over the still-working **MCG SRB** to the MN. The report can include the failure cause and SCG-related measurement information collected around the failure, allowing the network to diagnose the event.

Importantly, this is not triggered merely because SCG radio quality becomes poor. The UE reports only after the poor condition or another problem reaches a defined **failure declaration condition**. If the MCG has also failed, the UE cannot rely on the normal SCG-failure reporting path; it follows the MCG RLF/re-establishment behavior instead.

If upper layers abort the RRC connection establishment procedure because the NAS procedure was aborted, and the UE has not yet entered `RRC_CONNECTED`, the UE treats the establishment attempt as unsuccessful and abandons it.

In particular, the UE shall:

- stop the establishment timer `T300` (if running);
- reset MAC and release the MAC configuration associated with the attempted establishment;
- release any radio-bearer/lower-layer configuration created for the unsuccessful attempt, as applicable;
- discard the pending RRC connection-establishment context;
- transition/remain in `RRC_IDLE`;
- perform the applicable idle-mode cell-selection actions; and
- report the establishment failure/abort outcome to upper layers as applicable.

The UE does **not** wait for an `RRCSetup` after the NAS abort, and it does not send an `RRCRelease` to the network: an RRC connection was never established.

The **Counter Check procedure** allows the network to verify that the UE and gNB still have consistent **PDCP COUNT state** for selected DRBs.

PDCP COUNT, composed of the PDCP sequence number and higher-layer count bits, is security-relevant because it is used as an input to:

- user-plane ciphering;
- integrity protection where applicable; and
- PDCP replay protection.

In `RRC_CONNECTED`, typically after AS security has been established and when the network wants to validate state, the gNB can send `CounterCheck` for one or more DRBs. The UE returns a `CounterCheckResponse` containing its relevant COUNT-MSB information. The network compares the UE-reported values with its own PDCP state.

From a security perspective, this helps detect cases such as:

- UE/gNB PDCP state desynchronization;
- use of stale bearer state after mobility, recovery, or re-establishment-related situations;
- abnormal counter evolution potentially consistent with replay, loss/insertion of PDCP PDUs, or manipulation of bearer state;
- a situation in which ciphering could otherwise be applied with inconsistent COUNT inputs.

This is important because reusing, rolling back, or incorrectly synchronizing COUNT values can weaken the intended anti-replay and ciphering properties. For example, PDCP COUNT contributes to the ciphering input; a repeated or inconsistent COUNT under the same security context is undesirable and may indicate a security or state-management problem.

The procedure is not, by itself, a new authentication mechanism and does not magically identify every attacker. The CounterCheck messages are protected using the established AS security context, so an attacker without the keys should not be able to forge a valid response. Rather, the procedure gives the network a protected way to detect that the UE’s security-relevant PDCP state does not match the network’s expectation. On detecting a mismatch, the network can apply its policy, such as bearer recovery, re-establishment-related handling, release, or security-context/key management actions.

The UE declares **Event X2** to have occurred when the configured X2 **entering condition** is satisfied for the configured **timeToTrigger**.

In principle, X2 is a two-part condition:

1. The relevant serving/reference cell quality becomes worse than the configured **X2 threshold 1**; and  
2. The relevant candidate/neighbor cell quality becomes better than the configured **X2 threshold 2**.

Using the usual measurement-event notation, the entering condition is evaluated from the applicable *filtered* measurement quantities as:

\[
M_s + Hys < Thresh_{X2,1}
\]

and

\[
M_n + O_{fn} + O_{cn} - Hys > Thresh_{X2,2}
\]

where:

- \(M_s\) is the measured quantity of the serving/reference cell;
- \(M_n\) is the measured quantity of the candidate/neighbor cell;
- \(Thresh_{X2,1}\) and \(Thresh_{X2,2}\) are the thresholds configured in the Event X2 configuration;
- \(Hys\) is the configured hysteresis;
- \(O_{fn}\) and \(O_{cn}\) are, where applicable, the frequency-specific and cell-specific offsets for the neighbor/candidate cell.

The UE starts the `timeToTrigger` timer when **both** inequalities become true. If either condition ceases to be true before the timer expires, the timer is stopped/reset. Event X2 is considered to have occurred only when both conditions remain fulfilled continuously for the configured `timeToTrigger`.

After that, the UE performs the corresponding event-triggered measurement reporting according to the associated `ReportConfigNR`—for example, subject to the configured report interval, report amount, and reporting-cell rules.

Event X2 is an **event-triggered measurement-reporting condition for serving-cell deterioration**.

The UE monitors the configured serving-cell measurement quantity (for example, RSRP, RSRQ, or SINR). It considers Event X2 to be fulfilled when the serving-cell quality falls below the configured absolute X2 threshold, subject to the configured hysteresis and time-to-trigger. Conceptually:

\[
M_s + Hys < Thresh_{X2}
\]

where \(M_s\) is the serving-cell measurement result.

Its purpose is to notify the network that the UE’s current serving link has become poor, so the network can make a mobility or reconfiguration decision, such as preparing or initiating a change of serving cell/configuration.

Event X2:

- causes a **MeasurementReport** according to the applicable report configuration;
- is not itself a handover, reconfiguration, or radio-link-failure declaration;
- is distinct from RLF detection: it is an RRM/mobility quality indication, not a declaration that the connection has failed;
- may also be configured with reporting-on-leave behavior, so the UE can report when the X2 condition is no longer met.

A frequency being an **MBS frequency of interest** is driven by the UE’s interest in an MBS service and by the service’s applicability/availability on that frequency or in its service area—not by the instantaneous existence of a broadcast MRB on that frequency.

A broadcast MRB is a current NG-RAN radio-resource realization of an MBS session. NG-RAN may not currently have established such an MRB on a frequency because, for example:

- no interested UE is currently present in the relevant cells;
- the MBS session is temporarily not being transmitted over radio there;
- NG-RAN has not yet activated the radio delivery for that session on that frequency;
- the session may currently be served by another delivery mode, such as unicast or another MBS radio configuration;
- the UE is anticipating reception after mobility/reselection into cells on that frequency.

The UE can nevertheless regard the frequency as of interest when it wants the relevant MBS service and knows, e.g. from service/session information, system information, configuration, or service-area information, that the service can be provided there.

This distinction is intentional: UE interest information can be used by NG-RAN to make the delivery decision. In particular, the network may establish or activate a broadcast MRB on that frequency in response to UE interest, rather than requiring the MRB to already exist before the UE can express that interest.

When the UE detects that either the `messageIdentifier` or the `serialNumber` received in SIB7 is different from the currently stored values for SIB7, it shall treat the received SIB7 as belonging to a new ETWS secondary notification/warning message.

Accordingly, the UE shall:

- discard the previously stored SIB7 warning-message information/segments associated with the old identifier and serial number;
- replace the current SIB7 `messageIdentifier` and `serialNumber` with the newly received values;
- start storing and assembling the warning-message segments for the new SIB7 message, beginning with the received segment.

The UE must not combine segments from the old and new `(messageIdentifier, serialNumber)` combinations. Once all segments of the new warning message have been received, it provides the reassembled warning message to upper layers.

For Event A6, the UE reports when a **neighbour cell becomes sufficiently better than a configured/reference SCell**.

The Event A6 entry condition is evaluated using the configured trigger quantity (e.g. RSRP, RSRQ, or SINR), after the applicable L3 filtering and offsets have been applied.

Conceptually, the UE enters Event A6 when:

\[
M_n + O_{fn} + O_{cn} - Hys
>
M_s + O_{fs} + O_{cs} + Off_{A6}
\]

where:

- \(M_n\): measured quantity of the neighbour cell;
- \(M_s\): measured quantity of the relevant SCell;
- \(O_{fn}\), \(O_{fs}\): frequency-specific offsets for the neighbour and SCell;
- \(O_{cn}\), \(O_{cs}\): cell-specific offsets for the neighbour and SCell;
- \(Off_{A6}\): configured `a6-Offset`;
- \(Hys\): configured hysteresis.

Thus, a positive `a6-Offset` means that the neighbour must be better than the SCell by at least that offset, in addition to the effect of hysteresis.

The UE does not report merely because the inequality is momentarily true. It starts the configured `timeToTrigger` when the Event A6 entry condition becomes true. If the condition remains true continuously for the entire `timeToTrigger`, the UE initiates event-triggered measurement reporting for the corresponding `MeasId`.

After triggering:

- the UE sends measurement reports according to `reportInterval` and `reportAmount`;
- the report contains results according to the configured report quantities and cell/beam reporting rules;
- the gNB decides whether to add, remove, or change an SCell—the UE does not autonomously perform an SCell change because of A6.

If `reportOnLeave` is configured, the UE can also report when the event leaves. The leave condition uses hysteresis in the opposite direction, conceptually:

\[
M_n + O_{fn} + O_{cn} + Hys
<
M_s + O_{fs} + O_{cs} + Off_{A6}
\]

Event A6 is therefore a **relative neighbour-versus-SCell event**, unlike A4, which is an absolute neighbour threshold event, or A3, which compares a neighbour with the SpCell.

RRC is involved because sidelink discovery is not just a PHY/MAC transmission or reception task. It needs policy, authorization, and radio-resource configuration before the UE can announce discovery information or monitor for it.

In TS 38.331 terms, RRC provides the control-plane framework that tells the UE, for example:

- whether sidelink discovery is allowed/enabled for the UE;
- whether the UE is configured to perform announcement, monitoring, or both;
- which sidelink carrier/BWP and resource pool(s) are applicable;
- the time/frequency resources and associated parameters to use;
- the discovery-related identities, destination/service context, and filters relevant to monitoring;
- priority, QoS/policy restrictions, and any applicable timers or validity conditions;
- whether the configuration is network-provided, system-information based, or preconfigured.

The reason is that discovery activity must be coordinated with cellular resource management and operator policy. Without RRC control, a UE could transmit discovery announcements on inappropriate resources, monitor the wrong resources, or perform discovery when it is not authorized or when sidelink operation is not configured in that area.

RRC involvement is therefore mainly configuration and control:

- **RRC configures/enables** discovery announcement and/or monitoring.
- **MAC/PHY perform** the actual resource selection, transmission, reception, and decoding according to that RRC configuration.
- Higher layers use the discovered information to determine whether a relevant sidelink peer/service is available.

The applicable source of configuration depends on the UE’s RRC situation. In connected operation, the network can provide or modify dedicated sidelink/discovery configuration through RRC signaling. When no active dedicated RRC connection is available, the UE may rely on valid stored, broadcast, or preconfigured sidelink parameters, subject to the specification’s conditions.

Thus, RRC is involved to ensure that sidelink discovery is performed only under the configured conditions and using controlled radio resources; it does not itself carry out the physical discovery transmissions or receptions.

An NR sidelink measurement object defines the **measurement target** for sidelink operation: i.e., what sidelink carrier/reference signals the UE is to observe and measure.

In practical terms, it provides information such as the relevant sidelink frequency and the sidelink reference/synchronization signals to measure (for example SL-SSB and, where applicable, sidelink CSI-RS), together with associated radio parameters such as subcarrier spacing or signal/index restrictions.

Its role is to let the UE obtain sidelink radio-quality information, such as sidelink RSRP-type measurements, for purposes such as:

- selecting or reselecting a sidelink synchronization source;
- evaluating candidate sidelink peers, relays, or coverage/synchronization conditions;
- monitoring sidelink link quality;
- producing measurement reports when the corresponding report configuration requires it.

The measurement object itself defines **what to measure**, not **when to report it**. Reporting criteria, thresholds, events, periodicity, and the association of a measurement object with a report configuration are controlled through the related measurement-report configuration and measurement identity.

Also, configuring a sidelink measurement object does not itself allocate PSCCH/PSSCH resources, establish a PC5 connection, or grant sidelink transmission resources. It is a measurement definition used by the relevant sidelink measurement and mobility/selection procedures.

An RRC_CONNECTED UE may need to apply a new network-provided sidelink configuration because sidelink operation is subject to the serving network’s current radio-resource policy.

In NR sidelink, the network can use an RRC reconfiguration to add, modify, or release dedicated sidelink configuration for a UE. This is needed, for example, when the network must:

- enable sidelink for a UE that has indicated sidelink interest, traffic, destination information, or QoS requirements;
- provide or change the sidelink carrier, sidelink BWP, resource pools, or related physical-layer parameters;
- configure or revise resources for network-scheduled sidelink operation (Mode 1), including configured-grant-related operation where applicable;
- change the resource pools and restrictions within which a UE performs autonomous resource selection (Mode 2);
- adapt sidelink resources because of load, interference, congestion, coverage, priority traffic, or operator policy;
- update the configuration after mobility, serving-cell change, BWP/frequency changes, or a change in the UE’s sidelink service requirements;
- release old sidelink resources/configuration that must no longer be used.

Being in `RRC_CONNECTED` is important because the UE has a dedicated RRC signaling connection and the gNB can therefore provide UE-specific sidelink configuration dynamically, rather than relying only on common or preconfigured sidelink information.

The UE must not continue to use stale dedicated sidelink resources after the network has replaced or released them. Applying the new configuration ensures that its PSCCH/PSSCH transmission, reception, sensing, and resource selection/scheduling behavior remains aligned with the network’s current configuration.

This does **not** mean that every RRC_CONNECTED UE automatically transmits sidelink traffic. The new RRC sidelink configuration establishes or updates the radio conditions and permissions for sidelink; actual sidelink transmission still depends on the configured sidelink procedure, available resources, traffic, and applicable higher-layer conditions.

For a `BH-RLC-ChannelConfig` whose `bh-RLC-ChannelIdentity` is **not already configured in that cell group**, the IAB-node treats it as an **addition of a new BH RLC channel**, not as a modification or re-establishment of an existing one.

It shall:

- create/configure the BH RLC channel identified by that `bh-RLC-ChannelIdentity`;
- establish the corresponding RLC entity;
- apply the included `rlc-Config` to that RLC entity, if present;
- configure the associated MAC logical channel according to the included `logicalChannelConfig`, if present.

`reestablishRLC`, when present, is relevant to re-establishing an already existing RLC entity. For an identity not previously configured in the same cell group, the required behavior is to establish the new BH RLC channel/RLC entity and apply its supplied configuration.

An **MBS Interest Indication is not a request for access to an MBS service**, nor is it limited to the case where the service is unavailable to the UE.

A UE can already be able to receive an MBS service—for example, because the service is being broadcast in the serving cell—and still indicate its interest because the network may not otherwise know that this particular UE wants that service.

The indication gives the NG-RAN UE-specific demand information that it can use for such purposes as:

- deciding whether to start, retain, expand, or stop MBS transmission/resource allocation;
- selecting or adapting MBS delivery, e.g. broadcast versus multicast-oriented delivery/configuration;
- configuring or maintaining MBS-related radio resources for the UE;
- supporting mobility/service continuity, so that the network can consider the UE’s MBS interest when the UE moves or is reconfigured;
- collecting interest/load information for admission, resource, and coverage decisions.

Thus, “the UE can receive the service now” and “the network knows the UE is interested in the service” are different facts. Reception availability may be cell-wide and non-UE-specific, whereas the MBS Interest Indication explicitly reports the UE’s current upper-layer interest. Subject to the applicable RRC configuration and triggering conditions, the UE may therefore send it even while MBS reception is already possible or ongoing.

A `measObjectId` is not self-contained: it is an identifier used by a `MeasId` to bind a measurement object to a reporting configuration.

In TS 38.331, the UE treats the measurement configuration as a delta to its stored measurement configuration:

- A measurement object may already be stored from an earlier configuration, or may be created/modified by `measObjectToAddModList` in the received `MeasConfig`.
- A `MeasId` in `measIdToAddModList` is usable only if its referenced `measObjectId` resolves to a configured measurement object (and its `reportConfigId` resolves to a configured report configuration).

If the `measObjectId` referenced by a `MeasId` does not exist after applying the relevant measurement-object configuration, the UE considers that `MeasId` invalid/ignores it. Consequently:

- the UE does not perform measurements for that measurement identity;
- the UE does not evaluate measurement events or periodic reporting for it;
- the UE does not send measurement reports using that `measId`;
- valid measurement objects, report configurations, and other valid measurement identities in the same `MeasConfig` can still be processed.

This is not normally an ASN.1 decoding error—the ASN.1 type only constrains the numerical range of `measObjectId`; it cannot enforce that the referenced object exists. It is a semantic cross-reference error handled by making the affected measurement identity unusable, rather than by treating the identifier as an implicit measurement object or reporting against an arbitrary object.

The inclusion of `ue-TxTEG-AssociationList` is governed by the **UE positioning assistance information procedure** in TS 38.331.

In particular, the UE includes this list only when it is providing UE positioning assistance information in response to the network’s relevant positioning configuration/request for UE Tx timing-error-group (TxTEG) association reporting. The list reports the UE’s association of configured positioning SRS resources with UE TxTEG IDs.

Thus:

- it is a **positioning-assistance-information report**, not a normal measurement report or a generic `UEAssistanceInformation` indication;
- the UE includes it only if the network has requested/configured the TxTEG-association information and the UE has the applicable association information for the configured positioning resources;
- absent that request/configuration, the UE does not include `ue-TxTEG-AssociationList` merely because it is sending other positioning assistance information.

A UE in **RRC_IDLE with no serving cell** can perform NR sidelink operation in an **out-of-coverage (OoC)** scenario, provided that it has an applicable **NR sidelink preconfiguration**.

That preconfiguration is typically provisioned by upper layers (for example, pre-installed/provisioned or obtained while previously in coverage) and includes the sidelink carrier/resource-pool and related SL parameters needed for autonomous PC5 operation. The UE can then use the preconfigured sidelink resources, including autonomous resource selection where applicable, without requiring an RRC connection or current serving cell.

Thus, the key case is:

- UE is in `RRC_IDLE`;
- UE has **no serving cell** / is out of network coverage;
- UE has valid **SL preconfiguration** for NR sidelink;
- the UE performs PC5 sidelink communication using those preconfigured resources, subject to sidelink synchronization and resource-selection rules.

Without such preconfiguration, lack of a serving cell alone does not enable sidelink operation.

When an L2 U2N Relay UE receives a configuration containing `uu-Relay-RLC-ChannelToReleaseList`, it treats the list as a per-channel removal instruction.

For each `Uu-Relay-RLC-ChannelID` in the list, the relay UE:

- identifies the corresponding configured Uu-Relay RLC channel;
- releases the RLC entity/configuration associated with that channel identity;
- stops using that channel for relayed traffic on Uu, including its associated logical-channel and RLC handling;
- discards/removes the channel-specific RLC state and buffered data as part of RLC-entity release, rather than attempting to continue or re-establish traffic on that channel.

The release is limited to the identified **Uu-Relay-RLC channel(s)**. It is not, by itself:

- an RRC connection release;
- a release of all relay functionality;
- necessarily a release of unrelated Uu-Relay RLC channels or PC5-side resources.

If a channel identity is released and also provided in the add/modify configuration in the same reconfiguration, the normal release/add-or-modify processing permits the channel to be removed and then configured again according to the new parameters. The relay UE applies the resulting configuration and, where applicable, acknowledges the enclosing RRC reconfiguration with the normal RRC completion procedure; there is no separate RRC response specifically for each released Uu-Relay-RLC channel.

The network enables a UE to act as a **U2N Relay UE** by providing it with a dedicated sidelink relay configuration through RRC signaling.

In practice, the serving gNB sends an **`RRCReconfiguration`** to a UE in `RRC_CONNECTED`, carrying the NR sidelink dedicated configuration (`sl-ConfigDedicatedNR`). For Rel-17 U2N relay operation, this configuration includes **`sl-RelayUE-Config-r17`**.

When `sl-RelayUE-Config-r17` is configured, the UE is configured to operate as a Relay UE for the indicated relay service(s), identified by the configured relay service code(s). The configuration provides the relay-related sidelink settings needed for the UE to:

- advertise/discover its availability as a relay on PC5, as applicable;
- establish and maintain PC5 sidelink communication with Remote UE(s);
- receive traffic from Remote UE(s) over PC5;
- relay that traffic toward the network through its Uu connection, and relay downlink traffic from the network back to the Remote UE(s);
- use the configured sidelink radio resources and bearer/QoS-related configuration associated with the relay operation.

Thus, being sidelink-capable or having generic sidelink configuration alone does not make a UE a U2N Relay UE. The UE becomes network-authorized/configured for U2N relay operation when the network supplies the relay-specific configuration—particularly `sl-RelayUE-Config-r17`—via dedicated RRC reconfiguration. The network can subsequently modify or remove that configuration to change or stop the UE’s relay role.

In the TS 38.331 architecture, the UE RRC layer is the Access Stratum (AS) control-plane protocol that sits between NAS and the radio protocol stack. It does not carry normal user-plane traffic; instead, it establishes, configures, reconfigures, suspends/resumes, and releases the radio resources used by the other layers.

The interaction is logical/functional: TS 38.331 specifies RRC procedures and the resulting actions on other layers, rather than requiring a particular internal UE software API.

## 1. Interaction with NAS

RRC provides the radio-access control function used by NAS.

Key interactions include:

- **NAS-triggered access:** NAS can cause the UE to initiate RRC connection establishment or resume, for example for mobile-originated signalling or data.
- **NAS transport:** RRC transports NAS PDUs transparently between the UE NAS and the gNB:
  - Uplink, typically using `ULInformationTransfer`;
  - Downlink, typically using `DLInformationTransfer`.
- **Paging:** RRC receives paging from lower layers and processes the UE identity and paging cause. When appropriate, this results in NAS being informed so that the UE can perform the relevant service or mobility procedure.
- **State support:** RRC radio states support NAS operation:
  - In `RRC_IDLE`, the UE performs cell selection/reselection and monitors paging/system information.
  - In `RRC_INACTIVE`, the UE retains an RRC context in the network and can resume more efficiently.
  - In `RRC_CONNECTED`, RRC maintains dedicated radio resources and enables NAS signalling and user-plane bearers.

RRC does not interpret NAS protocol contents for mobility-management or session-management purposes. It carries NAS information and provides the radio connection needed for NAS procedures.

## 2. Interaction with PDCP

RRC signalling is normally transferred over **Signalling Radio Bearers (SRBs)**, for which PDCP provides functions such as:

- RRC PDU transfer;
- ciphering and integrity protection once AS security is activated;
- PDCP sequence-number handling and retransmission-related functions, where applicable.

RRC configures PDCP through RRC configuration procedures. For example, an RRC reconfiguration can establish or modify PDCP entities and configure parameters for SRBs and DRBs.

RRC also controls AS security activation. After receiving the relevant security configuration from the network, RRC causes the lower layers, notably PDCP, to apply ciphering and integrity protection using the derived AS keys.

Typical bearer usage is:

- **SRB0:** RRC messages on CCCH, used for initial access before dedicated signalling is available;
- **SRB1/SRB2** (and, where applicable, other SRBs): dedicated RRC and NAS-related signalling on DCCH;
- **DRBs:** user-plane bearers. RRC configures them, but user-plane packets normally pass through SDAP/PDCP/RLC/MAC/PHY without RRC processing each packet.

## 3. Interaction with RLC

RRC configures RLC entities associated with SRBs and DRBs. It can instruct the UE to establish, modify, re-establish, or release RLC entities and configure parameters such as:

- RLC mode, for example AM or UM;
- sequence-number size;
- retransmission and status-report related parameters;
- reassembly and reordering parameters.

RRC PDUs are delivered to and from RLC via the relevant logical channel. RLC provides segmentation, reassembly, and, in AM, acknowledged-mode reliability for the RRC signalling bearers that use it.

## 4. Interaction with MAC

RRC configures MAC behavior and provides the control information needed for MAC operation. This includes, for example:

- logical-channel configuration and priorities;
- scheduling-request configuration;
- buffer-status reporting configuration;
- random-access configuration;
- discontinuous reception (DRX) configuration;
- configured grants and other uplink-related configuration;
- cell-group and serving-cell related MAC configuration.

MAC is responsible for multiplexing logical channels into transport blocks, handling scheduling-related operation, random access support, HARQ, and transport to/from PHY.

For initial RRC access, MAC/PHY provide the random-access and common-channel transport needed for messages such as an RRC setup or resume request. RRC determines which procedure is required; MAC executes the configured radio-access mechanisms.

## 5. Interaction with PHY

RRC configures the physical-layer operation required for communication with the serving cell(s). This includes configuration related to:

- serving cells and bandwidth parts;
- downlink and uplink BWP operation;
- SIB-defined common physical configuration;
- PDCCH/PDSCH/PUSCH/PUCCH related configuration;
- CSI measurement and reporting configuration;
- SRS configuration;
- beam management and spatial-relation related configuration;
- measurement objects, reporting quantities, and measurement gaps;
- carrier aggregation, dual connectivity, and cell activation/deactivation-related configuration.

PHY supplies measurements and radio indications used by RRC, such as measurements supporting mobility, beam management, radio link monitoring, and reporting. RRC evaluates the configured measurement/reporting conditions and sends measurement reports to the network when their event or periodic conditions are satisfied.

## 6. Transport of RRC messages through lower layers

RRC messages are mapped to logical channels, then transported by lower layers:

| RRC usage | Logical channel | Typical purpose |
|---|---|---|
| Broadcast RRC information | BCCH | MIB and system information |
| Paging | PCCH | Paging information |
| Initial common RRC signalling | CCCH | Setup/resume/re-establishment related common signalling |
| Dedicated RRC and NAS transport | DCCH | Dedicated signalling over SRBs |

MAC maps these logical channels to the appropriate transport channels, and PHY transmits/receives them over the radio interface.

## 7. Overall role

RRC is therefore the control coordinator of the radio stack:

- it receives system information, paging, common and dedicated signalling from the lower layers;
- it exchanges NAS information with NAS;
- it uses SRBs through PDCP/RLC/MAC/PHY to send and receive dedicated RRC signalling;
- it configures PDCP, RLC, MAC, and PHY entities for SRBs and DRBs;
- it controls radio-resource procedures such as connection establishment, reconfiguration, mobility, measurement reporting, security activation, suspend/resume, and release.

In short, NAS decides or requests higher-level service and mobility actions, RRC translates the required radio-access actions into configurations and procedures, and PDCP/RLC/MAC/PHY implement the configured signalling and radio transport.

For every `drb-Identity` contained in `drb-ToReleaseList` that exists in the UE’s current radio-bearer configuration, the UE releases that DRB and its associated lower-layer resources. In particular, it shall release:

- the DRB identified by the `drb-Identity`;
- the associated PDCP entity;
- the associated RLC entity or entities, where applicable;
- the associated MAC logical channel(s), where applicable; and
- the associated SDAP entity, where applicable.

A listed DRB identity that is not part of the current configuration does not result in release of an existing bearer/resource. The release is applied as part of processing the received radio-bearer configuration; it is not a separate per-DRB RRC signalling transaction.
